 By Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown &middot; Oct 26, 2026 
 
 
 Friday's newsletter  — particularly Harvard economist Jason Furman's comments asserting that AI can already do much of the work of the White House's economic policy shop — drove a fascinating online conversation over the weekend. We have highlights below.

 But first, how the midterm politics of tariffs have turned distinctly against President Trump. Today's newsletter, edited by Jeffrey Cane and copy edited by Katie Lewis, is 1,156 words, a 4.5-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Trump&#x27;s midterm tariff test 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Sen. Susan Collins (R-Maine), speaking to the media in May. Photo: Graeme Sloan/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 With the midterm elections eight days away, Trump faces an uncomfortable political dilemma: how to stand by the tariffs at the heart of his economic agenda when Republican voters are less convinced of their benefits.

 Why it matters: Since returning to the White House, Trump has moved more aggressively than any modern president to remake the global trading system, with sweeping tariffs at the center of that effort.

 But with Americans already frustrated by high prices — including a wartime surge in energy prices — his signature economic policy threatens to become a political liability for Republicans fighting to retain control of Congress. Driving the news: In what is the clearest example yet of how midterm politics are complicating Trump's tariff agenda, the White House exempted certain Canadian imports from 50% tariffs following pressure from Sen. Susan Collins (R-Maine), who faces a tough reelection race.

 The exemptions cover agricultural machinery, plywood and packaging materials — offering relief to Maine farmers and businesses. Collins touted her role in securing the exemptions, pledging to seek further tariff relief and a broader trade agreement with Canada. Of note: The tariffs were not eliminated outright. 

 The administration swapped them for 50% duties on other Canadian goods — including carpets, shampoo, paint, cocoa powder, wooden coffins and more — in a bid to keep pressure on the Canadians during the trade spat. The big picture: Other Republican candidates across the country are walking a fine line — defending Trump's tariffs while distancing themselves from the parts of the trade agenda that are squeezing constituents.

 Iowa: The state's farmers union broke decades of neutrality in federal races to endorse the entire Democratic congressional slate — citing mounting frustration over Trump's trade policies and rising costs, as the New York Times reported . Michigan: Republican Senate candidate Mike Rogers told NBC's "Meet the Press" yesterday that the Canadian auto tariffs "worry me a little bit" and pledged to seek relief. "There's some good and there's some things we have to work on," Rogers said. Between the lines: Republicans are less optimistic about the benefits of the president's tariffs. 

 Forty percent expected the policies to have a "mostly positive" effect on the country in October — down from 49% in January and 52% in August 2025, according to Pew Research . A separate AP-NORC poll earlier this month showed that 64% of Americans believe Trump has gone too far in imposing new tariffs on other countries. The other side: "We're trying to create another American century here," Trump trade chief Jamieson Greer said in a Bloomberg profile published today.

 Greer noted that public discontent reflects "micro issues" unrelated to tariffs — including housing, health care, education and energy costs. What to watch: The economic benefits Trump promises from new factories and manufacturing jobs could take longer to materialize, while businesses and consumers are already feeling the higher costs associated with tariffs.

 At a campaign rally in Georgia on Saturday, Trump announced a $4.2 billion Audi factory — the company's first in the U.S. — that is expected to create 4,500 jobs. "Without tariffs, they wouldn't be coming in," he said. Audi cited other business considerations in its factory announcement beyond reducing exposure to tariffs, including proximity to U.S. customers and a more competitive production base. 
 
